.TH TETROMINO 6 "2026-09-24" "tetromino 1.1.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
tetromino \- falling-block puzzle game for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tetromino
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-difficulty
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-level
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-seed
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-theme
.IR THEME ]
.RB [ \-\-color
.IR MODE ]
.RB [ \-\-mono ]
.RB [ \-\-das
.IR MS ]
.RB [ \-\-arr
.IR MS ]
.RB [ \-\-legacy\-keys ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-demo ]
.RB [ \-\-fps
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.br
.B tetromino
.RB \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tetromino
is a falling-block puzzle game that runs in a terminal.
Seven kinds of four-square pieces fall into a well; move and rotate them to
fill complete rows, which then disappear.
The game ends when the stack reaches the top.
.PP
The start screen is a setup menu where you choose a mode, a board size and a
difficulty.
Your best score for each combination is kept.
If the menu is left alone for 15 seconds, a computer player starts a demo game;
press any key to return to the menu.
The board is drawn at the largest size that fits the terminal, and the
terminal is restored when the game exits, including on errors and signals.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " NAME"
Pre-select the mode:
.B endless
(default; play until the stack reaches the top) or
.B 2\-minute
(score as much as possible in two minutes of play).
.TP
.BI \-\-size " NAME"
Pre-select the board in the start menu:
.B small
(8\(mu16),
.B classic
(10\(mu20, default),
.B wide
(14\(mu20) or
.B tall
(10\(mu24).
.TP
.BI \-\-difficulty " NAME"
Pre-select the difficulty:
.BR easy ,
.B normal
(default),
.B hard
or
.BR expert .
See
.BR DIFFICULTY .
.TP
.BI \-\-level " N"
Start at level
.I N
(1\-30, default 1).
The difficulty may add further levels.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
Use a fixed random seed.
The same seed gives the same sequence of pieces on every machine.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " THEME"
.B dark
or
.B light
colours, or
.B auto
(default): ask the terminal for its background colour, then fall back to
.BR COLORFGBG ,
then dark.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " MODE"
Force the colour mode:
.BR truecolor ,
.BR 256 ,
.B 16
or
.BR mono .
By default it is detected from
.BR COLORTERM " and " TERM .
.TP
.B \-\-mono
Same as
.BR "\-\-color mono" .
.TP
.BI \-\-das " MS"
Delay before a held move key starts repeating (default 167 ms).
.TP
.BI \-\-arr " MS"
Time between repeated moves while a key is held (default 33 ms; 0 slides
straight to the wall).
Both apply only on terminals that report key releases (the kitty keyboard
protocol, supported by Kitty, Foot, WezTerm, Ghostty and Alacritty);
elsewhere the operating system's key repeat is used.
.TP
.B \-\-legacy\-keys
Use the operating system's key repeat even if the terminal reports key
releases.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-demo
Don't start the demo game when the menu is left idle.
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " N"
Maximum render rate, 10\-240 (default 60).
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Write a debug log (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of options and controls.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH CONTROLS
.SS Start menu
.TP 16
.BR \(ua " " \(da " or " W " " S
Choose the board or difficulty row.
.TP
.BR \(<- " " \(-> " or " A " " D
Change the selected option.
.TP
.B Enter
Start the game.
.TP
.BR Q " or " Esc
Quit.
.SS Playing
.TP 16
.BR \(<- " " \(-> " or " A " " D
Move the piece.
.TP
.BR \(ua ", " W " or " X
Rotate clockwise.
.TP
.B Z
Rotate counter-clockwise.
.TP
.BR \(da " or " S
Soft drop (one row).
.TP
.B Space
Hard drop.
.TP
.B C
Hold the current piece (if the difficulty allows it).
.TP
.BR P " or " Esc
Pause, and resume after a 3-2-1 countdown.
.TP
.B R
Restart (when paused or after game over).
.TP
.B M
Back to the setup menu (when paused or after game over).
.TP
.BR Q " or " Ctrl\-C
Quit.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
Suspend to the shell; resume with
.BR fg .
.SH DIFFICULTY
.TS
l l l l l l.
Difficulty	Fall speed	Start level	Hold	Next pieces	Ghost
Easy	1.5\(mu slower	+0	yes	5	yes
Normal	standard	+0	yes	3	yes
Hard	0.6\(mu	+2	no	1	yes
Expert	0.35\(mu	+4	no	0	no
.TE
.PP
The level rises every 10 cleared lines.
.SH SCORING
Points are multiplied by the level.
Clearing 1, 2, 3 or 4 lines at once scores 100, 300, 500 or 800.
A T piece rotated into a tight slot (at least three of the four corners
around its centre blocked) is a spin: 400 with no lines, 800, 1200 or 1600
with 1, 2 or 3 lines; a mini spin scores 100, 200 or 400.
A four-line clear or line-clearing spin that follows another one scores
\(mu1.5 (back-to-back).
Consecutive pieces that clear lines add 50 \(mu combo count.
Emptying the board adds 800, 1200, 1800 or 2000 (perfect clear).
Soft drops score 1 point per row and hard drops 2.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set to a non-empty value, disables colour.
.TP
.BR COLORTERM ", " TERM
Used to detect colour support.
.TP
.B TETROMINO_DEBUG
If set to a value other than 0, enables debug logging, like
.BR \-\-debug .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base directory for the debug log (default
.IR ~/.local/state ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/tetromino/highscores
Best score for each mode, board and difficulty.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/tetromino/tetromino.log
Debug log, written only with
.B \-\-debug
or
.BR TETROMINO_DEBUG .
.SH NOTES
The terminal must use UTF-8 and be at least 50\(mu20 for the menu and about
58\(mu22 for the classic board (the menu shows the exact size needed).
Colour terminals get 24-bit colour, with fallbacks to 256 and 16 colours.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on normal exit, 1 on errors (for example when not run in a terminal),
2 for invalid command-line options.
